  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Transformative Research for Adaption to Change in Informal Settlements (Urban TRACS)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Urban TRACS project &#x2013; also referred to as Transformative Research for Adaptation to Climate Change in Informal Settlements is a two-year project funded by the International Development Research Centre (IDRC) in Canada. The project aims to integrate a collaborative and participatory research approach to assess climate risk, impacts and vulnerabilities, existing capacities, systems and relationships for climate adaptation and inclusive resilience-enhancing actions for marginalised urban settlements in Kenya and Sierra Leone. The project leadership is provided by the Liverpool School of Tropical Medicine (LSTM) in London and the Slum Dwellers International in Kenya (SDI-Kenya). Other partners include The George Institute of Global Health (TGI) and Sierra Leone partners, including the Sierra Leone Urban Research Centre (SLURC), the Institute of Gender and Children&#x2019;s Health Research (IGCHR), and the Centre of Dialogue for Human Settlement and Poverty Alleviation (CODOHSAPA).</narrative>
  </description>
